{% load site_tags %}
{% comment %}
"Closing: <show>" — the mailing that goes out near the end of a run.

Same shape as show_opening.mjml so the two read as a pair, with the emphasis moved: the date that
matters here is the one the show comes down, and the works are given more room because by now the
reader has seen the headline image and what might bring them back is a piece they have not.

Everything comes from the Show row and its Events. The masthead, "Come visit" block, address and
unsubscribe link come from the shell in templates/email/campaign_base.mjml.

Available in context (gallery/campaigns.py::show_context):
  show, show_url, artworks, events, opening, closing, curators, site
  campaign_body / campaign_body_blocks — the curator's own words for this one, if any

`closing` is the show's last Event, and only exists when there is more than one — a show with a
single reception has an opening, not a finale, and this template must not present one as the
other.
{% endcomment %}
<mj-section padding="16px 24px 0">
  <mj-column>
    <mj-text font-size="13px" color="#888888" letter-spacing="1px" padding="0 0 4px">LAST CHANCE</mj-text>
    <mj-text font-size="28px" font-weight="bold" padding="0 0 10px">
      {% if closing %}Closing: {% endif %}{{ show.name }}
    </mj-text>
    {% comment %}
      The last day it is *open*, never the day it comes down. "Comes down on Saturday" leaves a
      reader unsure whether Saturday is too late, and next to a closing event on that same Saturday
      it reads as a contradiction — which is exactly how this was first noticed. Naming the last
      day you can see it has neither problem.

      One sentence per branch, unbroken, so each stays greppable and testable.
    {% endcomment %}
    {% if closing and closing.date == show.end %}
    <mj-text padding="0 0 10px">The last day to see {{ show.name }} is {{ show.end|date:"l, j F" }}, ending with {{ closing.name }}.</mj-text>
    {% elif closing %}
    <mj-text padding="0 0 10px">The last day to see {{ show.name }} is {{ show.end|date:"l, j F" }}, and {{ closing.name }} is on {{ closing.date|date:"l, j F" }}.</mj-text>
    {% else %}
    <mj-text padding="0 0 10px">The last day to see {{ show.name }} is {{ show.end|date:"l, j F" }}{% if site %} at {{ site.name }}{% endif %}.</mj-text>
    {% endif %}
  </mj-column>
</mj-section>

{% comment %}
  The same date/time/place block as the opening, so the pair are recognisably the same mail — but
  labelled, because on a closing the date needs to say which date it is. The event carries its own
  date only when it is not the last day, so the common case does not print it twice.
{% endcomment %}
<mj-section padding="0 24px 8px">
  <mj-column>
    <mj-text font-size="18px" line-height="1.7" padding="8px 0">
      📅 Last day: {{ show.end|date:"l, j F" }}<br />
      {% if closing %}🕓 {{ closing.name }}{% if closing.date != show.end %}, {{ closing.date|date:"l, j F" }}{% endif %}, {{ closing.time_range }}<br />{% endif %}
      📍 {% if maps_url %}<a href="{{ maps_url }}" style="color:#222222">{{ site_name }}</a>{% else %}{{ site_name }}{% endif %}
    </mj-text>
    {% if closing_calendar %}
    <mj-text font-size="14px" padding="0 0 10px">
      📅 Add to calendar:
      <a href="{{ closing_calendar.google }}" style="color:#198754">Google</a>
      &nbsp;·&nbsp;
      <a href="{{ closing_calendar.ics }}" style="color:#198754">Apple/Outlook</a>
    </mj-text>
    {% endif %}
  </mj-column>
</mj-section>

{% if show.image %}
<mj-section padding="8px 24px">
  <mj-column>
    <mj-image src="{{ show.image.url }}" alt="{{ show.name }}" padding="0" href="{{ show_url }}" />
  </mj-column>
</mj-section>
{% endif %}

{% comment %}
Whatever the curator wrote for this mailing. On a closing that is usually the reason to come
back — a piece that sold, a review, a talk added at the last minute. Placed above the show's
standing description, which the reader has probably had once already.
{% endcomment %}
{% if campaign.body_markdown.strip %}{{ campaign_body }}{% endif %}

{% if show.description %}
<mj-section padding="8px 24px">
  <mj-column>
    <mj-text padding="8px 0">{{ show.description|text_blocks|linebreaksbr|truncatewords_html:45 }}</mj-text>
  </mj-column>
</mj-section>
{% endif %}

<mj-section padding="0 24px 8px">
  <mj-column>
    <mj-button href="{{ show_url }}" background-color="#198754" color="#ffffff" padding="16px 0">
      See it before it goes
    </mj-button>
  </mj-column>
</mj-section>

{% if artworks %}
{% comment %}
  Six here rather than three, in two explicit slices. MJML needs each row to be its own section
  with its own columns, and Django's template language has no grouper — so spelling out two rows
  is both shorter than a clever loop and the only version that is obviously correct.
{% endcomment %}
<mj-section padding="0 24px 4px">
  {% for artwork in artworks|slice:":3" %}
  <mj-column width="33%">
    {% if artwork.card_md %}<mj-image src="{{ artwork.card_md.url }}" alt="{{ artwork.name }}" padding="4px" href="{{ show_url }}" />{% endif %}
    <mj-text font-size="13px" align="center" padding="4px" line-height="1.4">
      {{ artwork.name }}
      {% if artwork.artists.all %}<br /><span style="color:#888888">{% for a in artwork.artists.all %}{{ a.name }}{% if not forloop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}</span>{% endif %}
    </mj-text>
  </mj-column>
  {% endfor %}
</mj-section>
{% if artworks|length > 3 %}
<mj-section padding="0 24px 16px">
  {% for artwork in artworks|slice:"3:6" %}
  <mj-column width="33%">
    {% if artwork.card_md %}<mj-image src="{{ artwork.card_md.url }}" alt="{{ artwork.name }}" padding="4px" href="{{ show_url }}" />{% endif %}
    <mj-text font-size="13px" align="center" padding="4px" line-height="1.4">
      {{ artwork.name }}
      {% if artwork.artists.all %}<br /><span style="color:#888888">{% for a in artwork.artists.all %}{{ a.name }}{% if not forloop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}</span>{% endif %}
    </mj-text>
  </mj-column>
  {% endfor %}
</mj-section>
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
